  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Bulk of the area will see cooler temps through\nthe day today thanks to cloud coverage and rain. A small heat \nadvisory is in place for Duplin and Onslow Counties where the \nlikelihood of the Sun peaking through debris clouds from last \nnight's and today's convection will be the highest. AppT values \nin excess of 105deg are possible in these zones for a few hours\nfrom mid- day to early afternoon before another round of \nshowers and storms develops. While cooler, AppTs will still be\nin the upper 90s for areas along and N of the front, mainly N of\nHWY 70. TUE and WED, MaxTs climb back into the low to mid 90s \nfor most with less cloudiness on deck. Afternoon Tds \napproaching 80deg with these temps will bring heat headlines \nback into play.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There are several factors expected to support \nan increasingly active pattern into next week. \n\nThe stalled boundary, persistent inland troughing, and plenty \nof moisture influx from the S and SW will keep cloudy and active\nforecast in place. Fortunately, the mid and upper level trough \nover NECONUS will be pushing offshore, ending the little mid and\nupper level dynamic support system for organized convection. \nA fair amount of cloud coverage will dampen heating from what \nwe have experienced over the weekend when 4-6kJ/kg SBCAPEs were \nrealized almost every afternoon and evening, but we'll still get\ninto the 2-4kJ/kg range, which is nothing to scoff at. Iso \nstrong storms with damaging wind gusts will be possible. PWATs\nstill greater than 2in will keep threat for heavy rain which \ncould lead to localized flooding in place through tonight. \nTonight, the boundary lifts back N, \"warm- sectoring\" the FA \nonce again for TUE and WED. \n\nTUE features slight dip in available moisture with drier\n850-700mb layer getting pushed up the SECONUS coast ahead of the \nnext upper trough pushes SE across the OH River valley, leading\nto more typical summer-time PoPs of 20-40% through TUE night. \n\nA stronger front is forecast to approach and cross the area late WED\ninto THU, posing the next threat for severe weather later in \nthe forecast period. Upper level pattern for this FROPA appears \nmore supportive of stronger storms with a sharper trough aloft \ndigging further S than this weekend's trough. Moisture increases\nahead of this front with low to midlevel flow opening up\nmoisture advection channel from the E side of what is now\nTropical Depression 2. The National Hurricane Center continues \nissuing advisories for TD2, but poses no tropical threat to ENC\nat this time, as it is forecast to meander slowly W across the\nNern Gulf of America.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...WED/THU stronger front is forecast to make it\nthrough the entirety of the land area of ENC, but still stall\noffshore. This will lead to mostly N-NEerly flow late week into\nthe weekend, cutting down on the heat (mid to upper 80s/upper\n60s splits into the weekend). Severe threat appears minimal\nbehind the midweek front, but the multi day period of showers in\nthe forecast could lead to hydro issues.",
